<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  12.5 Lines and Planes in Space
-->
<section xml:id="chapter-lines-planes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Lines and Planes in Space</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Write the vector equation and the parametric equations of a line in
space from a point on the line and a vector parallel to it.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute the distance from a point to a line using the cross product.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the equation of a plane from a point on the plane and a normal
vector, and find the line of intersection of two planes.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute the distance from a point to a plane using the projection onto
the normal vector.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-lines-vector-eq">
<title>Vector Equation for a Line in Space</title>

<p>
Consider a line that passes through the point <m>P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m>
and is parallel to the vector <m>\mathbf v</m>. Then any arbitrary
point on the line can be obtained by adding a scalar multiple of
<m>\mathbf v</m> to the position vector of <m>P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m>
that we will denote by <m>\mathbf r_0</m>. This way we can write the
vector equation of the line as

<md xml:id="eq-lines-vector" number="yes">
\mathbf r(t) = \mathbf r_0 + t\,\mathbf v, \quad t \in \R,
</md>
</p>

<p>
where <m>\mathbf r(t)</m> is the position vector of an arbitrary point
on the line. See <xref ref="fig-lines-vector-eq"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lines-vector-eq">
<caption>
The line through <m>P_0</m> parallel to <m>\mathbf v</m>. For any point
<m>P</m> on the line, the vector <m>\overrightarrow{P_0P}</m> is parallel to
<m>\mathbf v</m>.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional coordinate system with a blue line passing through
two labelled points. The point P zero equals x zero comma y zero comma
z zero sits higher on the line, and the point P equals x comma y comma
z sits lower. A magenta vector from P zero to P lies along the line,
and a separate magenta vector v below the line points in the same
direction, illustrating that the vector P zero P is parallel to v.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=0.85]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) -- (4.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,5.2,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,4.2) node[above] {$z$};
  % the line through P0 with direction v = (1.1, 1.5, -1.05)
  \draw[mblue, very thick] (0.35,-0.75,3.55) -- (3.65,3.75,0.40);
  % points
  \fill (1.45,0.75,2.50) circle (2pt) node[above right=1pt] {$P_0=(x_0,y_0,z_0)$};
  \fill (2.55,2.25,1.45) circle (2pt) node[right=2pt] {$P=(x,y,z)$};
  % vector P0P along the line
  \draw[-stealth, magenta, very thick] (1.45,0.75,2.50) -- (2.55,2.25,1.45);
  \node[magenta] at (1.60,1.95,2.35) {$\overrightarrow{P_0P}$};
  % vector v below the line
  \draw[-stealth, magenta, very thick] (2.0,1.2,-0.4) -- (3.1,2.7,-1.45);
  \node[magenta, below] at (3.1,2.7,-1.45) {$\mathbf v$};
  % boxed remark
  \node[draw, fill=white, inner sep=3pt] at (0.7,4.3,-1.3) {$\overrightarrow{P_0P} \parallel \mathbf v$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-lines-vector-eq-interactive"/> lets you move <m>P_0</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m> and slide the parameter <m>t</m> to see the point
<m>\mathbf r(t) = \mathbf r_0 + t\,\mathbf v</m> travel along the line.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lines-vector-eq-interactive">
<caption>
The line <m>\mathbf r(t) = \mathbf r_0 + t\,\mathbf v</m>. Drag
<m>P_0</m> or <m>\mathbf v</m> to move the line, and change <m>t</m> to
move the point along it.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-lines-vector-eq" geogebra="msfaupsn" width="100%" aspect="50:41">
<description>
<p>
A GeoGebra applet showing a line in three-dimensional space through a
point <m>P_0</m> with a direction vector <m>\mathbf v</m>. Dragging the
point or changing the vector moves the line, and a parameter <m>t</m>
slides a point <m>P</m> along it, tracing the position vector
<m>\mathbf r(t) = \mathbf r_0 + t\,\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</description>
</interactive>
</figure>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-lines-parametric">
<title>Parametric Equations for a Line</title>

<p>
The vector equation <xref ref="eq-lines-vector"/> can be interpreted as
the following parametric equations, where
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2, v_3\rangle</m>:
<md xml:id="eq-lines-parametric" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
x \amp = x_0 + t\,v_1 \\
y \amp = y_0 + t\,v_2 \\
z \amp = z_0 + t\,v_3
\end{aligned}
\qquad t \in \R.
</md>
</p>

<example xml:id="example-lines-two-points">
<title>The line through two points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the vector equation and the parametric equations of the line that
passes through the points <m>P(2,0,0)</m> and <m>P(1,-1,1)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We begin by finding a vector parallel to the line by using the two
given points:

<md>
<mrow>\mathbf v = \overrightarrow{P_0P} \amp = \langle 1-2,\, -1-0,\, 1-0\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \langle -1, -1, 1\rangle</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
By <xref ref="eq-lines-vector"/>, the vector equation of the line is
<md>
\mathbf r(t) = \mathbf r_0 + t\,\mathbf v, \quad t \in \R
</md>
</p>

<p>
where <m>\mathbf v = \langle -1,-1,1\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf r_0 = \langle 2,0,0\rangle</m>, hence

<md>
<mrow>\mathbf r(t) \amp = \langle 2,0,0\rangle
+ t\langle -1,-1,1\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \langle 2-t,\, -t,\, t\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Equivalently,
<m>\mathbf r(t) = (2-t)\,\hat{\imath} - t\,\hat{\jmath} + t\,\hat{k}</m>.
By <xref ref="eq-lines-parametric"/>, the parametric equations of the
line are

<md>
<mrow>x \amp = 2 - t,</mrow>
<mrow>y \amp = -t,</mrow>
<mrow>z \amp = t,</mrow>
<mrow>t \amp \in \R.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lines-two-points">
<caption>
The line through <m>P_0(2,0,0)</m> and <m>P(1,-1,1)</m>. The vector
<m>\overrightarrow{P_0P} = \langle -1,-1,1\rangle</m> is parallel to the line.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional coordinate system with a blue line passing through
the point P zero at two comma zero comma zero on the x axis and
climbing toward the upper left. A short green vector from P zero along
the line represents the direction vector P zero P equals negative one
comma negative one comma one.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.05]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.6,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.8) node[above] {$z$};
  % ticks
  \node[below left=1pt] at (2,0,0) {$2$};
  \node[right] at (0,1,0) {$1$};
  % the line r(t) = (2-t, -t, t) from t=-1.2 to t=2.2
  \draw[mblue, very thick] (3.2,1.2,-1.2) -- (-0.2,-2.2,2.2);
  % P0 and P
  \fill[red] (2,0,0) circle (2pt) node[below right=3pt, red] {$P_0$};
  \fill[red] (1,-1,1) circle (2pt) node[above right=1pt, red] {$P$};
  % direction vector
  \draw[-stealth, olive, very thick] (2,0,0) -- (1,-1,1);
  \node[olive, above=2pt] at (1.5,-0.5,0.5) {$\overrightarrow{P_0P}$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-lines-distance">
<title>The Distance from a Point to a Line</title>

<p>
Our goal is to find the distance <m>d</m> of the point <m>S</m> from
the line <m>L</m>. As shown in <xref ref="fig-lines-dist-line"/>, if
<m>P</m> is any point on the line and <m>\theta</m> is the angle
between <m>\overrightarrow{PS}</m> and the direction vector <m>\mathbf v</m>,
then

<md>
d = \|\overrightarrow{PS}\|\left|\sin\theta\right|,
</md>
</p>

<p>
which can be written in terms of the cross product as

<md xml:id="eq-lines-distance" number="yes">
d = \frac{\|\overrightarrow{PS} \times \mathbf v\|}{\|\mathbf v\|}
= \frac{\|\overrightarrow{PS}\|\,\|\mathbf v\|\left|\sin\theta\right|}{\|\mathbf v\|}.
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lines-dist-line">
<caption>
The distance of the point <m>S</m> from the line <m>L</m> is
<m>d = \|\overrightarrow{PS}\|\left|\sin\theta\right|</m>, where <m>P</m> is any point on the
line.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-lines-dist">
<diagram dimensions="(380,240)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.6,-1.1,5.4,3.2)">
<line at="dl-line" endpoints="((-0.35,-0.55),(5.1,1.63))"
      stroke="black" thickness="3"/>
<line at="dl-ps" endpoints="((0.9,-0.05),(2.4,2.5))"
      stroke="#009E73" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<line at="dl-drop" endpoints="((2.4,2.5),(3.42,0.958))"
      stroke="#8B1A4A" dash="6 4" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="dl-v" v="(1.35,0.54)" tail="(2.6,-0.75)"
        stroke="magenta" thickness="3"/>
<point at="dl-S" p="(2.4,2.5)" fill="black"/>
<point at="dl-P" p="(0.9,-0.05)" fill="black"/>
<label at="dl-S-lab" anchor="(2.4,2.62)" alignment="n">
<m>S</m>
</label>
<label at="dl-P-lab" anchor="(0.85,-0.22)" alignment="s">
<m>P</m>
</label>
<label at="dl-L-lab" anchor="(5.15,1.75)" alignment="ne">
<m>L</m>
</label>
<label at="dl-theta" anchor="(1.42,0.28)" alignment="ne">
<m>\theta</m>
</label>
<label at="dl-d-lab" anchor="(3.15,1.95)" alignment="ne" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\|\overrightarrow{PS}\|\left|\sin\theta\right|</m>
</label>
<label at="dl-v-lab" anchor="(3.3,-0.6)" alignment="s" color="magenta">
<m>\mathbf v</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A solid black line L runs up and to the right. A point S floats above the line. A dashed green segment joins a point P on the line to S, making an angle theta with the line. A dashed dark red segment drops from S perpendicularly to the line; its length is the magnitude of P S times the absolute value of sine theta. A magenta vector v below the line shows the line's direction.">
  <annotation ref="dl-line" text="The line L."/>
  <annotation ref="dl-S" text="The point S whose distance from the line we want."/>
  <annotation ref="dl-ps" text="The segment from P to S, which makes an angle theta with the line."/>
  <annotation ref="dl-drop" text="The perpendicular distance d, equal to the magnitude of P S times the absolute value of sine theta."/>
  <annotation ref="dl-v" text="The direction vector v of the line."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-lines-distance">
<title>Distance from a point to a line</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the distance between the point <m>S(1,1,0)</m> and the line
with the vector equation
<m>\mathbf r(t) = (2-t)\,\hat{\imath} - t\,\hat{\jmath} + t\,\hat{k}</m>,
<m>t \in \R</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The vector parallel to the line is
<m>\mathbf v = \langle -1,-1,1\rangle</m> which has length
<m>\|\mathbf v\| = \sqrt3</m>. We also need a point on the line. For
<m>t = 0</m>, we have <m>P(2,0,0)</m> and hence
<m>\overrightarrow{PS} = \langle -1,1,0\rangle</m>.

<md>
<mrow>\overrightarrow{PS} \times \mathbf v \amp =
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
-1 \amp -1 \amp 1
\end{vmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp =
\begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}\hat{\imath}
- \begin{vmatrix} -1 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}\hat{\jmath}
+ \begin{vmatrix} -1 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp -1 \end{vmatrix}\hat{k}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath} + 2\hat{k}.</mrow>
</md>

<md>
\|\overrightarrow{PS} \times \mathbf v\| = \sqrt{1 + 1 + 4} = \sqrt6
</md>

Hence, by <xref ref="eq-lines-distance"/>,
<md>
d = \frac{\|\overrightarrow{PS} \times \mathbf v\|}{\|\mathbf v\|}
= \frac{\sqrt6}{\sqrt3} = \sqrt2.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-planes-equation">
<title>Equation of a Plane in Space</title>

<p>
In the past, we learned how to compute the normal vector
<m>\overrightarrow{n}</m> to a plane. Let us assume that we have computed this
vector and that we have a point <m>P_0</m> on the plane. We know that a
normal vector is perpendicular to the plane and hence it is
perpendicular to any vector in the plane. Let us denote an arbitrary
point on the plane by <m>P</m>, then

<md xml:id="eq-planes-normal" number="yes">
\overrightarrow{n} \cdot \overrightarrow{P_0P} = 0.
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-planes-normal">
<caption>
The normal vector to a plane is perpendicular to all the vectors in the
plane.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<description>
<p>
A parallelogram represents a plane seen in perspective. A red vector n
points straight up from a point P zero on the plane, and a blue vector
lies in the plane from P zero to another point P. A small right angle
marker at P zero shows that the two vectors are perpendicular.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.0]
  % the plane
  \fill[msky, opacity=0.25] (-1.2,-1.6,0) -- (2.6,-1.6,0) -- (2.6,3.0,0) -- (-1.2,3.0,0) -- cycle;
  \draw[msky!70!black] (-1.2,-1.6,0) -- (2.6,-1.6,0) -- (2.6,3.0,0) -- (-1.2,3.0,0) -- cycle;
  % points
  \fill (0.7,0.3,0) circle (2pt) node[below left=1pt] {$P_0$};
  \fill (1.3,1.9,0) circle (2pt) node[below right=1pt] {$P$};
  % in-plane vector
  \draw[-stealth, mblue, very thick] (0.7,0.3,0) -- (1.3,1.9,0);
  % normal vector
  \draw[-stealth, red, very thick] (0.7,0.3,0) -- (0.7,0.3,2.3)
        node[above] {$\overrightarrow{n}$};
  % right-angle marker, built from the actual in-plane direction (0.6,1.6,0)
  % normalised, so that one leg lies along the blue vector
  \draw[semithick] (0.815,0.607,0) -- (0.815,0.607,0.35) -- (0.7,0.3,0.35);
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
If <m>P_0(x_0,y_0,z_0)</m>, <m>P(x,y,z)</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{n} = \langle A, B, C\rangle</m>, then the above equation gives
us

<md xml:id="eq-planes-point-normal" number="yes">
A(x - x_0) + B(y - y_0) + C(z - z_0) = 0,
</md>
</p>

<p>
which can also be re-written in the form

<md xml:id="eq-planes-standard" number="yes">
Ax + By + Cz = D,
</md>
</p>

<p>
where <m>D = Ax_0 + By_0 + Cz_0</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-planes-equation">
<title>Finding the equation of a plane</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation of the plane through the point <m>P_0(2,4,-1)</m>
with normal vector <m>\overrightarrow{n} = \langle 2,3,4\rangle</m>. Find the
intercepts and sketch the plane.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Substituting into the point-normal form
<xref ref="eq-planes-point-normal"/>:

<md>
<mrow>A(x-x_0) + B(y-y_0) + C(z-z_0) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>2(x-2) + 3(y-4) + 4(z+1) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>2x + 3y + 4z \amp = 12</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Setting two of the variables equal to zero at a time gives the
intercepts: the <m>x</m>-intercept is <m>(6,0,0)</m>, the
<m>y</m>-intercept is <m>(0,4,0)</m>, and the <m>z</m>-intercept is
<m>(0,0,3)</m>. The portion of the plane in the first octant is
sketched in <xref ref="fig-planes-intercepts"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-planes-intercepts">
<caption>
The plane <m>2x + 3y + 4z = 12</m> meets the coordinate axes at
<m>(6,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,4,0)</m>, and <m>(0,0,3)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional coordinate system in which a shaded triangle
represents the portion of the plane two x plus three y plus four z
equals twelve in the first octant. The triangle's vertices are the
intercepts six comma zero comma zero on the x axis, zero comma four
comma zero on the y axis, and zero comma zero comma three on the z
axis, with dashed lines marking the triangle's edges along the
coordinate planes.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=0.80]
  % axes
  % the axis label sits above the axis, opposite the (6,0,0) label below it
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) -- (7.8,0,0) node[above left=1pt] {$x$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,5.4,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,4.2) node[above] {$z$};
  % intercept triangle
  \fill[msky, opacity=0.35] (6,0,0) -- (0,4,0) -- (0,0,3) -- cycle;
  \draw[msky!60!black, thick, dashed] (6,0,0) -- (0,4,0);
  \draw[msky!60!black, thick, dashed] (0,4,0) -- (0,0,3);
  \draw[msky!60!black, thick, dashed] (0,0,3) -- (6,0,0);
  % intercept labels
  \fill (6,0,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below right=2pt] {$(6,0,0)$};
  \fill (0,4,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below=4pt] {$(0,4,0)$};
  \fill (0,0,3) circle (1.6pt) node[above right=1pt] {$(0,0,3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-planes-intersection">
<title>Lines of Intersection</title>

<p>
Suppose we would like to find the line of intersection <m>L</m> of two
planes with normal vectors <m>\overrightarrow{n}_1</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{n}_2</m>. To write down the equation of <m>L</m> we
need a direction vector <m>\mathbf v</m> for it, and the two normal
vectors are enough to produce one.
</p>

<p>
First, notice that <em>each normal vector is perpendicular to
<m>L</m></em>. By <xref ref="eq-planes-normal"/>, the normal vector
<m>\overrightarrow{n}_1</m> is orthogonal to every line in the first
plane. The line <m>L</m> lies in that plane, and therefore
<m>\overrightarrow{n}_1 \perp \mathbf v</m>. The line <m>L</m> lies in
the second plane as well, so the same argument with
<m>\overrightarrow{n}_2</m> gives
<m>\overrightarrow{n}_2 \perp \mathbf v</m>. These two right angles are
marked in <xref ref="fig-planes-intersection"/>.
</p>

<p>
So we are looking for a vector orthogonal to both
<m>\overrightarrow{n}_1</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{n}_2</m> <mdash/> and
by <xref ref="def-cross-product"/> that is exactly what the cross
product delivers. Provided the planes are not parallel, the normal
vectors are not parallel either, so
<m>\overrightarrow{n}_1 \times \overrightarrow{n}_2 \ne \mathbf 0</m>.
A direction vector is only determined up to a nonzero scalar multiple,
so we may take
<md xml:id="eq-planes-intersection-dir" number="yes">
\mathbf v = \overrightarrow{n}_1 \times \overrightarrow{n}_2.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Then, we can use it together with any common point to the two planes
to find the equation of the line of intersection. See
<xref ref="fig-planes-intersection"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-planes-intersection">
<caption>
The cross product of the normal vectors gives the direction vector of
the line of intersection.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
Two planes intersect inside a three dimensional box: a horizontal blue
plane and a slanted pink plane, meeting at an oblique angle rather than
a right angle. Both normal vectors leave the same marked point on the
line where the planes meet. The blue vector n one points straight up,
perpendicular to the blue horizontal plane; the pink vector n two points
up and to the side, perpendicular to the pink slanted plane. A black vector
runs along the crease where the planes meet. A legend in the upper right
names the three vectors: n one, n two, and v equals n one cross n two. A
square corner mark on either side of the common point, drawn in the colour
of its normal, shows that each normal makes a right angle with that line.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.32cm,-0.66cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=0.85]
  % box
  \draw[gray!60] (0,0,0) -- (4,0,0) -- (4,5,0) -- (0,5,0) -- cycle;
  \draw[gray!60] (0,0,4) -- (4,0,4) -- (4,5,4) -- (0,5,4) -- cycle;
  \draw[gray!60] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,4);
  \draw[gray!60] (4,0,0) -- (4,0,4);
  \draw[gray!60] (4,5,0) -- (4,5,4);
  \draw[gray!60] (0,5,0) -- (0,5,4);
  % horizontal plane at z = 1.9, drawn in the colour of its normal n_1
  \fill[msky!60!black, opacity=0.16] (0.3,0.3,1.9) -- (3.7,0.3,1.9) -- (3.7,4.7,1.9) -- (0.3,4.7,1.9) -- cycle;
  \draw[msky!60!black] (0.3,0.3,1.9) -- (3.7,0.3,1.9) -- (3.7,4.7,1.9) -- (0.3,4.7,1.9) -- cycle;
  % slanted plane through the same crease, tilted 35 degrees from the vertical
  % so the two planes meet at an oblique angle; drawn in the colour of n_2
  \fill[mpink!85!black, opacity=0.16] (0.3,3.318,0.589) -- (3.7,3.318,0.589) -- (3.7,1.482,3.211) -- (0.3,1.482,3.211) -- cycle;
  \draw[mpink!85!black] (0.3,3.318,0.589) -- (3.7,3.318,0.589) -- (3.7,1.482,3.211) -- (0.3,1.482,3.211) -- cycle;
  % line of intersection
  \draw[black, very thick] (0.3,2.4,1.9) -- (3.7,2.4,1.9);
  % both normals leave the SAME point of the crease, marked with a dot
  \fill[black] (1.6,2.4,1.9) circle (1.8pt);
  \draw[-stealth, msky!60!black, very thick] (1.6,2.4,1.9) -- (1.6,2.4,3.3);
  \draw[-stealth, mpink!85!black, very thick] (1.6,2.4,1.9) -- (1.6,3.547,2.703);
  % right angles with the line of intersection: equal legs of 0.5, one mark on
  % each side of the common point so that neither hides the other
  \draw[msky!60!black, semithick] (2.1,2.4,1.9) -- (2.1,2.4,2.4) -- (1.6,2.4,2.4);
  \draw[mpink!85!black, semithick] (1.1,2.4,1.9) -- (1.1,2.810,2.187) -- (1.6,2.810,2.187);
  % direction vector v along the crease
  \draw[-stealth, black, very thick] (1.6,2.4,1.9) -- (3.1,2.4,1.9);
  % legend; the font is set so that it prints at body-text size once the
  % picture is scaled to the width given in the <image> element
  \node[anchor=north east, draw=gray!60, rounded corners=1.5pt, fill=white,
        fill opacity=0.92, text opacity=1, inner sep=2.5pt,
        font=\fontsize{6}{7}\selectfont]
    at (current bounding box.north east) {%
    \begin{tabular}{@{}c@{\;}l@{}}
      \tikz[x=1cm,y=1cm,baseline=-0.5ex]{\draw[-stealth, msky!60!black, very thick] (0,0) -- (0.36,0);}
        & $\overrightarrow{n}_1$ \\[1.5pt]
      \tikz[x=1cm,y=1cm,baseline=-0.5ex]{\draw[-stealth, mpink!85!black, very thick] (0,0) -- (0.36,0);}
        & $\overrightarrow{n}_2$ \\[1.5pt]
      \tikz[x=1cm,y=1cm,baseline=-0.5ex]{\draw[-stealth, black, very thick] (0,0) -- (0.36,0);}
        & $\mathbf v = \overrightarrow{n}_1 \times \overrightarrow{n}_2$
    \end{tabular}};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-planes-intersection">
<title>The line of intersection of two planes</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the parametric equations of the line of intersection of the two
planes <m>3x - 6y - 2z = 15</m> and <m>2x + y - 2z = 5</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The two normal vectors are <m>\overrightarrow{n}_1 = \langle 3,-6,-2\rangle</m>
and <m>\overrightarrow{n}_2 = \langle 2,1,-2\rangle</m>, so by
<xref ref="eq-planes-intersection-dir"/>,
<md>
\mathbf v = \overrightarrow{n}_1 \times \overrightarrow{n}_2 =
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{\imath} \amp \hat{\jmath} \amp \hat{k} \\
3 \amp -6 \amp -2 \\
2 \amp 1 \amp -2
\end{vmatrix}
= 14\,\hat{\imath} + 2\,\hat{\jmath} + 15\,\hat{k}
</md>
</p>

<p>
We have found the direction of the line so far and now we need to find
a common point between the two planes. After substituting <m>z = 0</m>
into the equations of the planes we get

<md>
<mrow>3x - 6y \amp = 15 \amp
x - 2y \amp = 5 \amp
-2x + 4y \amp = -10</mrow>
<mrow>2x + y \amp = 5 \amp
2x + y \amp = 5 \amp
2x + y \amp = 5</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Adding the last pair of equations gives <m>5y = -5</m>, hence

<md>
\begin{cases}
y = -1 \\
x = 3
\end{cases}
</md>
</p>

<p>
Hence, the point <m>P_0(3,-1,0)</m> is in common between the two
planes. Finally, by <xref ref="eq-lines-parametric"/>, the parametric
equations of the line of intersection are

<md>
<mrow>x \amp = 3 + 14t,</mrow>
<mrow>y \amp = -1 + 2t,</mrow>
<mrow>z \amp = 15t,</mrow>
<mrow>t \amp \in \R.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-planes-distance">
<title>Distance of a Point from a Plane</title>

<p>
Our goal here is to find the distance <m>d</m> of the point <m>S</m>
from the plane. To do so we need a point <m>P</m> on the plane and the
normal vector to the plane <m>\overrightarrow{n}</m>. As shown in
<xref ref="fig-planes-distance"/>, the distance can be found by
projecting the vector <m>\overrightarrow{PS}</m> in the direction of
<m>\overrightarrow{n}</m> and then finding its magnitude:

<md>
<mrow>d = \left\|\operatorname{proj}_{\overrightarrow{n}}\overrightarrow{PS}\right\|
\amp = \left\|\left(\frac{\overrightarrow{PS}\cdot\overrightarrow{n}}{\|\overrightarrow{n}\|^2}\right)
\overrightarrow{n}\right\|
= \frac{|\overrightarrow{PS}\cdot\overrightarrow{n}|}{\|\overrightarrow{n}\|^2}\,\|\overrightarrow{n}\|</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{\left|\overrightarrow{PS}\cdot\overrightarrow{n}\right|}{\|\overrightarrow{n}\|}</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<assemblage xml:id="summary-plane-distance">
<title>Distance from a point to a plane</title>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-planes-distance" number="yes">
d = \frac{\left|\overrightarrow{PS}\cdot\overrightarrow{n}\right|}{\|\overrightarrow{n}\|}
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<figure xml:id="fig-planes-distance">
<caption>
The distance of the point <m>S</m> from the plane is the magnitude of
the projection of <m>\overrightarrow{PS}</m> onto the normal vector
<m>\overrightarrow{n}</m>. The corner mark records that the dashed
segment meets the plane at a right angle, so it is parallel to
<m>\overrightarrow{n}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
A parallelogram represents a plane seen in perspective. From a point P
on the plane, a blue normal vector n points straight up and a green
vector points up and to the right to a point S above the plane. A
dashed dark red segment drops vertically from S to the plane; its
length d is the distance from S to the plane, which equals the
magnitude of the projection of P S onto n. A small square corner mark
where the dashed segment meets the plane shows that the segment is
perpendicular to the plane.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}, scale=1.0]
  % the plane
  \fill[msky, opacity=0.20] (-1.4,-1.8,0) -- (2.8,-1.8,0) -- (2.8,3.2,0) -- (-1.4,3.2,0) -- cycle;
  \draw[msky!70!black] (-1.4,-1.8,0) -- (2.8,-1.8,0) -- (2.8,3.2,0) -- (-1.4,3.2,0) -- cycle;
  % points
  \fill (0.4,-0.4,0) circle (2pt) node[below left=1pt] {$P$};
  \fill (1.4,1.8,2.4) circle (2pt) node[above=2pt] {$S$};
  \fill (1.4,1.8,0) circle (1.6pt);
  % normal vector at P
  \draw[-stealth, mblue, very thick] (0.4,-0.4,0) -- (0.4,-0.4,1.9)
        node[above] {$\overrightarrow{n}$};
  % PS vector
  \draw[-stealth, mgreen, very thick] (0.4,-0.4,0) -- (1.4,1.8,2.4);
  % vertical distance
  \draw[mred, dashed, thick] (1.4,1.8,2.4) -- (1.4,1.8,0);
  % right angle: the dashed segment meets the plane perpendicularly
  \draw[mred, semithick] (1.4,1.8,0) -- (1.4,2.25,0) -- (1.4,2.25,0.45) -- (1.4,1.8,0.45);
  \node[mred, right=2pt] at (1.4,1.9,1.95)
        {$d = \left\|\operatorname{proj}_{\overrightarrow{n}}\overrightarrow{PS}\right\|$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-planes-distance">
<title>Distance from a point to a plane</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the distance from the point <m>S(2,3,0)</m> to the plane with
equation <m>5x + y + z = -1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We need a point <m>P</m> on the plane. We choose <m>x = y = 0</m>,
which when substituted into the equation of the plane
<m>5x + y + z = -1</m>, gives <m>z = -1</m>. Hence, we have a point
<m>P(0,0,-1)</m> on the plane. We then compute the vector
<m>\overrightarrow{PS} = \langle 2,3,1\rangle</m>. Note that we also can extract
the normal vector <m>\overrightarrow{n} = \langle 5,1,1\rangle</m>,
<m>\|\overrightarrow{n}\| = \sqrt{27}</m> from the equation of the plane,
which is in the standard form <xref ref="eq-planes-standard"/>.

<md>
\overrightarrow{PS}\cdot\overrightarrow{n} = 10 + 3 + 1 = 14
</md>
Substituting into <xref ref="eq-planes-distance"/> gives
<md>
d = \frac{\left|\overrightarrow{PS}\cdot\overrightarrow{n}\right|}{\|\overrightarrow{n}\|}
= \frac{14}{\sqrt{27}}
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

</section>
